    "https://www.lr.edu/work-at-lr/visiting-professor-chemistry": {
      "title": "Visiting Professor, Chemistry",
      "description": "Visiting Professor, Chemistry | Lenoir-Rhyne University Jump to Header Jump to Main Content Jump to Footer Return to home Open Menu Open Search Search Search Close Search Admission & Aid Click to toggle child links Undergraduate Admission Graduate Admission Seminary Admission Financial Aid & Costs LR Tuition-Free Guarantee College Affordability Paying Your Bill Academics Click to toggle child links Undergraduate Programs Graduate & Doctoral Programs Seminary Programs BEAR Central & Academic Support Research & Scholarship Study Abroad & International Education Internships & Careers Centers & Institutes Registrar Library Student Experience Click to toggle child links Activities & Organizations Athletics Health & Wellness Residence Life Leadership & Service Religious & Spiritual Life Campus Safety Dean of Students Belonging at LR Student Support & Outreach LR Experience Athletics About Click to toggle child links Fast Facts History & Traditions University Leadership Strategic Plan Accreditation Directory Offices & Services Community Partners Resources for Alumni Students Faculty & Staff Parents & Families Apply Visit Request Info Give Close Menu Visiting Professor, Chemistry Location : Hickory, North Carolina Faculty Position : One-semester, nine-month, non-tenure track position with the possibility of extension for additional years. Appointment begins January 2027. Position Summary The College of Natural Sciences and Mathematics at Lenoir-Rhyne University invites applications for a Visiting Professor of Chemistry to teach introductory, freshman-level courses in organic, biochemical or physical chemistry. This position is intended for a student-centered educator who is committed to providing undergraduate students with a strong foundation in the fields of chemistry. This position is a five-month teaching contract involving coursework for undergraduate students in the spring semester, with the potential for additional teaching assignments. A minimum of a master’s degree in chemistry or a closely related field is required, but a terminal degree is preferred. The degree must be awarded prior to the start date of this position, Jan. 4, 2026. Teaching will include courses in organic, biochemical and/or physical chemistry, along with evaluating student success and achievement and providing timely student feedback within those courses. Evidence of exemplary college teaching experience and the ability to develop and deliver both lecture and laboratory courses is highly preferred. Candidates must be able to conduct their assigned courses of instruction in the best manner possible to achieve student learning, which includes presenting ideas in ways that are appropriate to a variety of learning styles and demonstrate sensitivity to a diverse student population. Successful candidates will display a commitment and capacity for teaching excellence, student mentorship, collaborative work, scholarly and professional engagement and a dedication to strong institution-wide citizenship. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Teach undergraduate chemistry courses, potentially including general chemistry, organic chemistry, biochemistry and physical chemistry. Prepare and deliver engaging lectures, laboratory activities and other instructional experiences. Provide appropriate assessment of student learning through examinations, assignments, laboratory reports, projects, and other methods. Maintain accurate course records and communicate course expectations, learning outcomes, and student progress clearly. Provide appropriate academic support through office hours and individual student consultations. Maintain appropriate laboratory safety standards and ensure compliance with university and departmental policies. All position requirements outlined above reflect the minimum knowledge, skills, and abilities necessary to perform the role competently. This description is not intended to be exhaustive. Faculty may be assigned additional responsibilities and are expected to acquire new skills as needed. Lenoir-Rhyne University reserves the right to modify position responsibilities as appropriate. Required Qualifications A master’s degree or doctorate in Chemistry, Biochemistry or a closely related field from an accredited institution. Demonstrated ability or potential to teach undergraduate chemistry effectively. Preferred Qualifications A Ph.D. in Chemistry, Biochemistry or a related discipline. College-level teaching experience in one or more of the following areas: general chemistry, organic chemistry, biochemistry and/or physical chemistry. Experience teaching laboratory courses. Experience working at a small college or undergraduate-focused institution. Compensation Lenoir-Rhyne University offers a competitive salary and benefits package. Salary transparency is a priority; detailed information will be provided to candidates during the interview process. Application Process While completing the application, candidates will be prompted to submit a: Letter of Application, specifically discussing how the candidate meets the needs of the position Curriculum Vitae, including the names and addresses of three references Statement of Teaching Philosophy as it relates to LR’s Teaching Scholar Model The university’s mission, vision, and values statements may be reviewed using About LR . Questions regarding the position or search process may be directed electronically to Dr. Scott Schaefer , at scott.schaefer@lr.edu . Lenoir-Rhyne University promotes a welcoming campus environment that is integral to the university's commitment to excellence in engagement and education. We are particularly interested in candidates who have demonstrated experience nurturing a welcoming community, working with faculty, staff and/or students from all walks of life and/or incorporating varied perspectives and traditions in their current or previous positions. This commitment is rooted in our Lutheran tradition of fostering wisdom, nurturing vocation and preparing students for lives of purpose while serving the common good. About the College of Natural Sciences and Mathematics The College of Natural Sciences and Mathematics provides current and practical knowledge and skills that prepares students for a successful STEM career. The college offers eleven undergraduate degrees: biochemistry, biology, chemistry, computer science (including a cyber-security track), engineering physics (system integration & automation and traditional focuses), environmental science and policy, information technology, mathematics, medical studies, pre-engineering, and pre-medical science. At the graduate level, the college offers an MS in Sustainability Studies. About Lenoir-Rhyne University Lenoir-Rhyne University is a co-educational, private, comprehensive institution founded in 1891, with its traditional campus located in Hickory, North Carolina, a city of more than 40,000. The university has additional graduate centers in Asheville, N.C., and Columbia, S.C. Situated in the foothills of the Blue Ridge Mountains, Lenoir- Rhyne is less than an hour’s drive from Charlotte, and the Hickory-Lenoir-Morganton Metropolitan Statistical Area has a population of about 365,000. The university now offers more than 45 undergraduate and 20 graduate degree programs to approximately 2,400 students. Lenoir-Rhyne University seeks to liberate mind and spirit, clarify personal faith, foster physical wholeness, build community, and promote responsible leadership for service to the world. Affiliated with the Evangelical Lutheran Church in America (ELCA), Lenoir-Rhyne is open to people from all religious and non-religious backgrounds. Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity Policy Lenoir-Rhyne University is committed to providing a workplace and educational environment, as well as other benefits, programs, and activities that are free from discrimination, harassment and retaliation. LR is committed to creating a safe campus environment for all members of the LR community, including, but not limited to, those who identify as transgender and gender non-conforming. To that end, LR does not discriminate on the basis of race, religion, color, sex (including sex stereotypes and sex characteristics), ethnicity, national origin, physical or mental disability, age, marital status, pregnancy or related conditions, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran or military status or any other characteristic or status protected by applicable law. To ensure compliance with federal and state civil rights laws and regulations and to affirm its commitment to promoting the goals of fairness and equity in all aspects of the educational program, activity and employment, LR has developed internal policies and procedures that provide a prompt, fair and impartial process for those involved in an allegation of discrimination or harassment, and for allegations of retaliation. LR values and upholds the equal dignity of all members of its community and strives to balance the rights of the parties in the grievance process during what is often a difficult time for all those involved. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Apply Name First Name Last Name Email Application Documents Upload Curriculum Vitae One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Upload Letter of Application One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Upload Statement of Teaching Philosophy One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Upload Three Professional References One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Submit Leave this field blank Get Info Visit Apply Deposit Return to home Find us on Facebook Subscribe on YouTube Follow us on Instagram 625 7th Ave NE, Hickory, NC 28601 828.328.1741 Directory Virtual Tour, Maps & Directions Events Calendar University News Profile Magazine Work at LR Library Bookstore Copyright Privacy Policy Accessibility Title IX Safety & Emergency Preparedness Consumer Information Coronavirus © 2026 Lenoir-Rhyne University"
    },
    "https://www.lr.edu/work-at-lr/visiting-professor-biology-human-anatomy-and-physiology": {
      "title": "Visiting Professor, Human Anatomy and Physiology",
      "description": "Visiting Professor, Human Anatomy and Physiology | Lenoir-Rhyne University Jump to Header Jump to Main Content Jump to Footer Return to home Open Menu Open Search Search Search Close Search Admission & Aid Click to toggle child links Undergraduate Admission Graduate Admission Seminary Admission Financial Aid & Costs LR Tuition-Free Guarantee College Affordability Paying Your Bill Academics Click to toggle child links Undergraduate Programs Graduate & Doctoral Programs Seminary Programs BEAR Central & Academic Support Research & Scholarship Study Abroad & International Education Internships & Careers Centers & Institutes Registrar Library Student Experience Click to toggle child links Activities & Organizations Athletics Health & Wellness Residence Life Leadership & Service Religious & Spiritual Life Campus Safety Dean of Students Belonging at LR Student Support & Outreach LR Experience Athletics About Click to toggle child links Fast Facts History & Traditions University Leadership Strategic Plan Accreditation Directory Offices & Services Community Partners Resources for Alumni Students Faculty & Staff Parents & Families Apply Visit Request Info Give Close Menu Visiting Professor, Human Anatomy and Physiology Location : Hickory, North Carolina Faculty Position : One-semester, nine-month, non-tenure track position with the possibility of extension for additional years. Appointment begins January 2027. Position Summary The College of Natural Sciences and Mathematics at Lenoir-Rhyne University invites applications for a Visiting Professor of Human Anatomy and Physiology to teach introductory, freshman-level courses in human anatomy and physiology. This position is intended for a student-centered educator who is committed to providing undergraduate students with a strong foundation in the structure and function of the human body. This position is a five-month teaching contract involving coursework for undergraduate students in the spring semester, with the potential for additional teaching assignments. A minimum of a master’s degree in biology or a closely related field is required, but a terminal degree is preferred. The degree must be awarded prior to the start date of this position, Jan. 4, 2026. Teaching will include courses in anatomy and physiology, along with evaluating student success and achievement and providing timely student feedback within those courses.” Evidence of exemplary college teaching experience and the ability to develop and deliver courses both in person and online is highly preferred. Candidates must be able to conduct their assigned courses of instruction in the best manner possible to achieve student learning, which includes presenting ideas in ways that are appropriate to a variety of learning styles and demonstrate sensitivity to a diverse student population. Successful candidates will display a commitment and capacity for teaching excellence, student mentorship, collaborative work, scholarly and professional engagement and a dedication to strong institution-wide citizenship. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Teach freshman-level Human Anatomy and Physiology courses, including lecture and laboratory sections as assigned. Present foundational concepts in human anatomy and physiology. Develop and assess examinations, laboratory assignments, quizzes, projects and other student learning activities. Maintain accurate course records and communicate course expectations, learning outcomes, and student progress clearly. Provide appropriate academic support through office hours and individual student consultations. Maintain appropriate laboratory safety standards and ensure compliance with university and departmental policies. All position requirements outlined above reflect the minimum knowledge, skills, and abilities necessary to perform the role competently. This description is not intended to be exhaustive. Faculty may be assigned additional responsibilities and are expected to acquire new skills as needed. Lenoir-Rhyne University reserves the right to modify position responsibilities as appropriate. Required Qualifications Master’s degree or higher in anatomy, physiology, biology, human biology, biomedical sciences or a closely related discipline from an accredited institution. Demonstrated knowledge of human anatomy and physiology. Ability to teach introductory undergraduate anatomy and physiology courses. Teaching Expectations Translate complex anatomical and physiological concepts into accessible material for beginning college students. Compensation Lenoir-Rhyne University offers a competitive salary and benefits package. Salary transparency is a priority; detailed information will be provided to candidates during the interview process. Application Process While completing the application, candidates will be prompted to submit a: Letter of Application, specifically discussing how the candidate meets the needs of the position Curriculum Vitae, including the names and addresses of three references Statement of Teaching Philosophy as it relates to LR’s Teaching Scholar Model The university’s mission, vision, and values statements may be reviewed using About LR . Questions regarding the position or search process may be directed electronically to Dr. Scott Schaefer , at scott.schaefer@lr.edu . To ensure full review, completed applications must be submitted by October 31, 2026 and the search will continue until the position is filled. Lenoir-Rhyne University promotes a welcoming campus environment that is integral to the university's commitment to excellence in engagement and education. We are particularly interested in candidates who have demonstrated experience nurturing a welcoming community, working with faculty, staff and/or students from all walks of life and/or incorporating varied perspectives and traditions in their current or previous positions. This commitment is rooted in our Lutheran tradition of fostering wisdom, nurturing vocation and preparing students for lives of purpose while serving the common good. About the College of Natural Sciences and Mathematics The College of Natural Sciences and Mathematics provides current and practical knowledge and skills that prepares students for a successful STEM career. The college offers eleven undergraduate degrees: biochemistry, biology, chemistry, computer science (including a cyber-security track), engineering physics (system integration & automation and traditional focuses), environmental science and policy, information technology, mathematics, medical studies, pre-engineering, and pre-medical science. At the graduate level, the college offers an MS in Sustainability Studies. About Lenoir-Rhyne University Lenoir-Rhyne University is a co-educational, private, comprehensive institution founded in 1891, with its traditional campus located in Hickory, North Carolina, a city of more than 40,000. The university has additional graduate centers in Asheville, N.C., and Columbia, S.C. Situated in the foothills of the Blue Ridge Mountains, Lenoir- Rhyne is less than an hour’s drive from Charlotte, and the Hickory-Lenoir-Morganton Metropolitan Statistical Area has a population of about 365,000. The university now offers more than 45 undergraduate and 20 graduate degree programs to approximately 2,400 students. Lenoir-Rhyne University seeks to liberate mind and spirit, clarify personal faith, foster physical wholeness, build community, and promote responsible leadership for service to the world. Affiliated with the Evangelical Lutheran Church in America (ELCA), Lenoir-Rhyne is open to people from all religious and non-religious backgrounds. Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity Policy Lenoir-Rhyne University is committed to providing a workplace and educational environment, as well as other benefits, programs, and activities that are free from discrimination, harassment and retaliation. LR is committed to creating a safe campus environment for all members of the LR community, including, but not limited to, those who identify as transgender and gender non-conforming. To that end, LR does not discriminate on the basis of race, religion, color, sex (including sex stereotypes and sex characteristics), ethnicity, national origin, physical or mental disability, age, marital status, pregnancy or related conditions, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran or military status or any other characteristic or status protected by applicable law. To ensure compliance with federal and state civil rights laws and regulations and to affirm its commitment to promoting the goals of fairness and equity in all aspects of the educational program, activity and employment, LR has developed internal policies and procedures that provide a prompt, fair and impartial process for those involved in an allegation of discrimination or harassment, and for allegations of retaliation. LR values and upholds the equal dignity of all members of its community and strives to balance the rights of the parties in the grievance process during what is often a difficult time for all those involved. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Apply Name First Name Last Name Email Application Documents Upload Curriculum Vitae One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Upload Letter of Application One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Upload Statement of Teaching Philosophy One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Upload Three Professional References One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Submit Leave this field blank Get Info Visit Apply Deposit Return to home Find us on Facebook Subscribe on YouTube Follow us on Instagram 625 7th Ave NE, Hickory, NC 28601 828.328.1741 Directory Virtual Tour, Maps & Directions Events Calendar University News Profile Magazine Work at LR Library Bookstore Copyright Privacy Policy Accessibility Title IX Safety & Emergency Preparedness Consumer Information Coronavirus © 2026 Lenoir-Rhyne University"
    },
    "https://www.lr.edu/work-at-lr/teaching-assistant-professor-counseling": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant Professor, Counseling",
      "description": "Teaching Assistant Professor, Counseling | Lenoir-Rhyne University Jump to Header Jump to Main Content Jump to Footer Return to home Open Menu Open Search Search Search Close Search Admission & Aid Click to toggle child links Undergraduate Admission Graduate Admission Seminary Admission Financial Aid & Costs LR Tuition-Free Guarantee College Affordability Paying Your Bill Academics Click to toggle child links Undergraduate Programs Graduate & Doctoral Programs Seminary Programs BEAR Central & Academic Support Research & Scholarship Study Abroad & International Education Internships & Careers Centers & Institutes Registrar Library Student Experience Click to toggle child links Activities & Organizations Athletics Health & Wellness Residence Life Leadership & Service Religious & Spiritual Life Campus Safety Dean of Students Belonging at LR Student Support & Outreach LR Experience Athletics About Click to toggle child links Fast Facts History & Traditions University Leadership Strategic Plan Accreditation Directory Offices & Services Community Partners Resources for Alumni Students Faculty & Staff Parents & Families Apply Visit Request Info Give Close Menu Teaching Assistant Professor, Counseling Location : Asheville, North Carolina (Asheville Graduate Center) Faculty Position : Full-time, nine-month, non-tenure track position The College of Education & Human Services at Lenoir-Rhyne University seeks applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track Teaching Assistant Professor of Counseling beginning Aug 10, 2026. This nine-month appointment is housed within the School of Counseling and Human Services and serves the University’s CACREP-accredited M.A. in Counseling program. This position is contingent upon final budgetary approval. All faculty positions at Lenoir-Rhyne University carry a 24-credit annual teaching load, with the possibility of reassigned time for administrative responsibilities such as clinical coordination. Successful candidates will demonstrate a strong commitment to teaching excellence, student mentorship, scholarly and professional engagement, and active participation in campus service and citizenship. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Teach graduate-level courses in the CACREP-accredited M.A. in Counseling program. Maintain regular in-person office hours at the Asheville Graduate Center. Contribute to program, college, and university service and leadership as appropriate. Collaborate with LR’s Graduate Admissions Office to support enrollment and recruitment efforts. Develop and sustain strong partnerships with clinical placement sites. Engage in scholarly and professional activities consistent with faculty expectations. Participate in curriculum development and program assessment activities aligned with CACREP standards. All position requirements outlined above reflect the minimum knowledge, skills, and abilities necessary to perform the role competently. This description is not intended to be exhaustive. Faculty may be assigned additional responsibilities and are expected to acquire new skills as needed. Lenoir-Rhyne University reserves the right to modify position responsibilities as appropriate. Required Qualifications Terminal degree in Counseling and/or Counselor Education from a CACREP-accredited institution. Ability to teach in person at the Asheville Graduate Center. Commitment to maintaining regular office hours and fulfilling service or leadership responsibilities on site. For fall 2026 hire eligibility, official confirmation of successful dissertation defense must be submitted by July 31, 2026. Preferred Qualifications Credentials and experience to teach courses in Clinical Mental Health Counseling and/or School Counseling. Evidence of exemplary college-level teaching effectiveness. Experience teaching in multiple instructional modalities, including in-person and online formats. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with admissions teams and external clinical partners. Commitment to fostering a supportive, student-centered learning environment. Compensation Lenoir-Rhyne University offers a competitive salary and comprehensive benefits package. Salary details will be shared with candidates during the interview process. Application Process To complete the online application, visit: http://www.lr.edu/employment . To ensure full consideration, completed applications must be submitted by March 20, 2026. The search will continue until the position is filled. Applicants should submit: Letter of application specifically addressing qualifications and experience Current curriculum vitae or resume Statement of teaching philosophy List of three professional references (including name, current position, email, address, and phone number) The University’s mission, vision, and values statements may be reviewed using About LR . Questions regarding the position or search process may be directed electronically to the search committee chair, Dr. Astra Czerny, at astra.czerny@lr.edu . Lenoir-Rhyne University promotes a welcoming campus environment that is integral to the university's commitment to excellence in engagement and education. We are particularly interested in candidates who have demonstrated experience nurturing a welcoming community, working with faculty, staff and/or students from all walks of life and/or incorporating varied perspectives and traditions in their current or previous positions. This commitment is rooted in our Lutheran tradition of fostering wisdom, nurturing vocation and preparing students for lives of purpose while serving the common good. About the College of Education and Human Services The College of Education & Human Services provides research-based instruction and intensive clinical experiences that prepare students for impactful careers serving individuals and communities. The college offers undergraduate programs in elementary education, middle grades education, secondary education, and human and community services. At the graduate level, the college offers master’s degrees in teaching, human services, clinical mental health counseling, and school counseling. About Lenoir-Rhyne University Lenoir-Rhyne University is a co-educational, private, comprehensive institution founded in 1891, with its traditional campus located in Hickory, North Carolina, a city of more than 40,000. The university has additional graduate centers in Asheville, N.C., and Columbia, S.C. Situated in the foothills of the Blue Ridge Mountains, Lenoir- Rhyne is less than an hour’s drive from Charlotte, and the Hickory-Lenoir-Morganton Metropolitan Statistical Area has a population of about 365,000. The university now offers more than 45 undergraduate and 20 graduate degree programs to approximately 2,400 students. Lenoir-Rhyne University seeks to liberate mind and spirit, clarify personal faith, foster physical wholeness, build community, and promote responsible leadership for service to the world. Affiliated with the Evangelical Lutheran Church in America (ELCA), Lenoir-Rhyne is open to people from all religious and non-religious backgrounds. Non-Discrimination and Equal Opportunity Policy Lenoir-Rhyne University is committed to providing a workplace and educational environment, as well as other benefits, programs, and activities that are free from discrimination, harassment and retaliation. LR is committed to creating a safe campus environment for all members of the LR community, including, but not limited to, those who identify as transgender and gender non-conforming. To that end, LR does not discriminate on the basis of race, religion, color, sex (including sex stereotypes and sex characteristics), ethnicity, national origin, physical or mental disability, age, marital status, pregnancy or related conditions, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, veteran or military status or any other characteristic or status protected by applicable law. To ensure compliance with federal and state civil rights laws and regulations and to affirm its commitment to promoting the goals of fairness and equity in all aspects of the educational program, activity and employment, LR has developed internal policies and procedures that provide a prompt, fair and impartial process for those involved in an allegation of discrimination or harassment, and for allegations of retaliation. LR values and upholds the equal dignity of all members of its community and strives to balance the rights of the parties in the grievance process during what is often a difficult time for all those involved. Visa sponsorship is not available for this position. Apply Name First Name Last Name Email Application Documents Upload Curriculum Vitae One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Upload Letter of Application One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Upload Statement of Teaching Philosophy One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Upload Three Professional References One file only. 256 MB limit. Allowed types: pdf  doc  docx. Submit Leave this field blank Get Info Visit Apply Deposit Return to home Find us on Facebook Subscribe on YouTube Follow us on Instagram 625 7th Ave NE, Hickory, NC 28601 828.328.1741 Directory Virtual Tour, Maps & Directions Events Calendar University News Profile Magazine Work at LR Library Bookstore Copyright Privacy Policy Accessibility Title IX Safety & Emergency Preparedness Consumer Information Coronavirus © 2026 Lenoir-Rhyne University"
